ENTRY            23147   20110824   20120705   20120704       2228
SUBENT        23147001   20110824   20120705   20120704       2228
BIB                 17        114
TITLE      Measurement of Charged-Particle Emission Double-
           Differential Cross Section of Fluorine for 14.2 MeV
           Neutrons.
AUTHOR     (K.Kondo, I.Murata, K.Ochiai, N.Kubota, H.Miyamaru,
           C.Konno, T.Nishitani)
INSTITUTE  (2JPNJAE) K.Kondo, K.Ochiai, N.Kubota, C.Konno,
                     T.Nishitani.
           (2JPNOSA) I.Murata, H.Miyamaru.
REFERENCE  (J,NST,48,(8),1146,2011)
REL-REF    (M,22949001,K.Kondo+,J,NIM/A,568,723,2006)
            Details of method.
           (R,23000001,K.Kondo+,J,NST,45,(2),103,2008)
           Data for alpha-particle emission for carbon.
INC-SOURCE (D-T) Pencil DT-neutron beam. 350 keV, 20 mA deuteron
           beam bombards a large rotating tritium target.
           Generated neutrons are collimated by 2-m-thick
           shielding structure with narrow 2-cm-diameter hole.
           Neutron beam was approximately 25mm in diameter at the
           sample position.
SAMPLE      16-mm-wide, 30-mm-long, 50-mm-thick Teflon ([CF2]n)
           foil was fixed at the center of the vacuum chamber,
           which was set at 90 cm downstream from the outlet of
           the collimator. The accurate sample width (measured
           with 20-microm-resolution optical scanner)
           16.00+-0.04 mm.
FACILITY   (FNS,2JPNJAE) Fusion neutronics Source (FNS) facility.
DETECTOR   (TELES,SIBAR) Two sets of counter-telescope system with
           pair of deltaE-E silicon surface barrier detectors.
                                 dE-detector E-detector
                                 Telescope 1 - for alphas
           Sensitive thickness   9.6 microm  769 microm
           Active area           50 mm**2    300 mm**2
                                 Telescope 2 - for p,d,t
           Sensitive thickness   20 microm   1.500 microm
           Active area           200 mm**2   200 mm**2
           The energy resolutions for 5.486 keV alpha-particles
           from Am-241 source were 83 keV in telescope 1 and
           78 keV in telescope 2.
           Detection threshold for p -1.4 MeV, d - 1.8 MeV,
           t - 2.0 MeV, alphas - 3.6 MeV.
           Telescope 1:
           - distance between sample and dE-detector - 8.3 cm,
           - solid angle of telescope - 7.4E-3 for 90 deg angle of
           measurement, 7.3E-3 sr - for other angles;
           - sample orientation relative to neutron beam - 0 deg
           at 30 and 150 deg angles of measurement, - 40 deg at
           50,70,90,110,130 deg angles of measurement.
           Telescope 2:
           - distance between sample and dE-detector - 12.5 cm for
           15 deg angle of measurement, - 9.0 cm for other angles;
           - solid angle of telescope - 4.0E-3 for 15 deg angle of
           measurement, 7.8E-3 sr for 90 deg, 7.7E-3 sr - for
           other angles;
           - sample orientation relative to neutron beam - 0 deg
           at 15, 30, 150 deg angles of measurement, - 40 deg at
           50,70,90,110,130 deg angles of measurement.
           Angular resolution function was calculated by Monte
           Carlo method, given on Fig.2 of J,NST,48,(8),1146,2011.
           (FISCH) Neutron flux was supplementary monitored with
           U-238 fission chamber at approximately 5m downstream
           from the vacuum chamber during the measurement.
MONITOR    ((MONIT)13-AL-27(N,A)11-NA-24,,SIG) For incident
           neutron fluence.
MONIT-REF  (,,3,JENDL-D-99,,1999) JENDL Dosimetry File 99 .
CORRECTION -The contribution of alpha-particles emitted from
           carbon was subtracted by using our previously measured
           alpha-particle emission DDX data.
           The proton, deuteron, triton emission cross sections of
           carbon are so small at 14.2 MeV that the measured
           spectra only originated from fluorine.
           - For energy loss in the sample material by using
           SRIM-2003 code for response function (representing
           the effect of energy loss) calculation and spectrum
           unfolding procedure (code based on the spectrum-type
           Bayes estimation method ) with the calculated response
           function.
METHOD     (EDE)
ERR-ANALYS    Determination of neutron flux with Al foil :
           (ERR-1,0.9,1.7) Statistics of gamma ray count 0.9-1.7;
           (ERR-2) Detector efficiency;
           (ERR-3,,0.1) Al foil weight < 0.1;
           (ERR-4,,0.1) Uncertainty of irradiation, cooling and
           measurement  time < 0.1;
           (ERR-5,,0.1)Self-absorption of gamma-ray in Al foil
                       < 0.1;
           (ERR-6,,0.1) Correction factor due to coincidence sum
                       < 0.1;
           (MONIT-ERR) Standard Al-27(n,alpha) reaction cross
                      section;
              Charged-particle measurement and data processing:
           (ERR-7,,1.) Atomic density of the sample < 1.;
           (ERR-8,,0.5) Area of the irradiated sample < 0.5;
           (ERR-9,,0.5) Uncertainty due to particle discrimination
                       < 0.5;
           (ERR-10,,1.) Area of the dE-detector < 1.;
           (ERR-11,,1.) Distance of the sample and the dE detector
                      < 1.;
           (ANG-ERR) Detection angle of emitted charged particle;
           Error of response function was not considered.
           (ERR-SYS) Total systematic error 4.
           (ERR-S)        Standard deviation of the energy spectra
           obtained through unfolding (considering the Poisson
           distribution) was regarded as the propagating
           statistical error of the measurement.
            6-15% for angular-differential cross sections.
             Uncertainty due to the incomplete coverage of the
           angular range when charged-particle production cross
           section was deduced was not considered.
ADD-RES    (COMP) Data were compared with JENDL-3.3 and
           ENDF/B-VII.0 .
STATUS     (APRVD) Approved by author Keitaro Kondo 13.02.2012 .
HISTORY    (20110824A) M.M.
           (20120206R) Data were received from Keitaro Kondo
            and inserted in data Subents.
ENDBIB             114
COMMON               7          6
EN         EN-ERR     MONIT      MONIT-ERR  ERR-2      ANG-ERR
ERR-SYS
MEV        MEV        MB         PER-CENT   PER-CENT   ADEG
PER-CENT
 14.2       0.2        120.4       0.5       2.0       1.
 4.
ENDCOMMON            6
ENDSUBENT          124
SUBENT        23147004   20110824   20120705   20120704       2228
BIB                  4          6
REACTION   (9-F-19(N,X)1-H-3,,DA/DE)
PART-DET   (T)
EN-SEC     (E,T)
             ANG - angle in lab.system.
STATUS     (TABLE) From author K.Kondo.
                   Data are on Fig.8 of J,NST,48,(8),1146,2011.
ENDBIB               6
